          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nHigh blood pressure, also called hypertension, means the pressure in your arteries stays raised over time, not just on one occasion. Blood pressure is written as two numbers, measured in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). The top number is the pressure when the heart beats, and the bottom number is the pressure between beats. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nPressure changes throughout the day with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, and sleep. Because of this, one high reading is not enough for a diagnosis. The standard is an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Home readings or a 24-hour monitor can show whether the raised pressure is consistent. They can also reveal \"white-coat hypertension,\" where readings are high only at the doctor's office, and \"masked hypertension,\" where office readings look normal but readings at other times are high. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, cardiology society guidance.)\n\nHypertension usually causes no symptoms, so it is most often found during routine checks. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n\nThe materials I searched did not give the exact numbers that define a diagnosis. If you have a reading you are worried about, or want to know whether your readings count as high, ask a clinician, who can take repeat measurements and advise you.",
